1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,705
Section eight, the end of part one.

2
00:00:02,985 --> 00:00:10,643
We build the layers that turn clean data into answers: an intermediate model with our business rules, and the marts analysts use.

3
00:00:10,924 --> 00:00:14,358
The biggest agent task so far, so we plan before we act.

4
00:00:15,458 --> 00:00:16,653
Staging is done.

5
00:00:16,933 --> 00:00:25,978
The intermediate layer adds a trip ID, removes exact duplicates, and adds trip duration, fare per mile, and the valid trip flag we promised.

6
00:00:26,258 --> 00:00:32,104
The marts are a fact table of valid trips, a zone dimension, and a daily summary per pickup zone.

7
00:00:33,204 --> 00:00:35,593
The structure guide on intermediate models.

8
00:00:35,873 --> 00:00:41,420
Each one has a specific purpose, like isolating complex logic so the marts stay simple.

9
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,492
End users never query them.

10
00:00:43,772 --> 00:00:45,414
They are views or ephemeral.

11
00:00:46,514 --> 00:00:47,709
The guide on marts.

12
00:00:47,989 --> 00:00:53,216
A mart is something the business understands: here, a trip, a zone, and a zone per day.

13
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:56,738
Marts are wide, and they are tables, so queries are fast.

14
00:00:57,838 --> 00:01:01,145
Materialization means how dbt builds a model.

15
00:01:01,425 --> 00:01:03,110
A view stores only the query.

16
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:05,012
A table stores the result.

17
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:08,300
Incremental adds only new rows, in section ten.

18
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:10,905
Ephemeral builds nothing and is inlined.

19
00:01:11,185 --> 00:01:14,662
A materialized view is kept up to date by the database.

20
00:01:15,762 --> 00:01:17,469
The materializations page.

21
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:23,701
You can set them in the project file, in YAML, or in the model itself, and the most specific setting wins.

22
00:01:23,981 --> 00:01:28,717
We set them per folder, and in section ten, one model will override its folder.

23
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:32,569
This task touches four models and several files.

24
00:01:32,849 --> 00:01:37,606
If the agent starts writing and gets the grain wrong, we review a lot of wrong code.

25
00:01:37,886 --> 00:01:40,681
So first, we ask for a plan, and we fix the plan.

26
00:01:41,781 --> 00:01:51,189
In the demo, the first prompt asks for the plan only: which intermediate and mart models, the grain of each one, its inputs and its materialization.

27
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:54,690
And it must wait for our OK, without writing any files.

28
00:01:55,790 --> 00:01:57,348
This is the plan we approve.

29
00:01:57,628 --> 00:02:00,166
One intermediate model, one row per trip.

30
00:02:00,446 --> 00:02:02,836
A fact table, one row per valid trip.

31
00:02:03,116 --> 00:02:04,289
A zone dimension.

32
00:02:04,569 --> 00:02:07,300
And a daily summary, one row per zone per day.

33
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:10,246
Fixing a plan is much cheaper than fixing code.

34
00:02:11,346 --> 00:02:12,477
Then: go ahead.

35
00:02:12,757 --> 00:02:22,122
The agent builds the trip ID, removes duplicates, adds duration and fare per mile with our macro, the validity flag with a reason, and the code names.

36
00:02:22,402 --> 00:02:26,839
Then the marts, YAML with tests for every model, and one dbt build.

37
00:02:27,939 --> 00:02:29,411
The heart of the project.

38
00:02:29,691 --> 00:02:32,017
The surrogate key from dbt utils.

39
00:02:32,297 --> 00:02:33,641
Duration in minutes.

40
00:02:33,921 --> 00:02:35,947
Fare per mile with our safe divide.

41
00:02:36,227 --> 00:02:37,806
One row per trip ID.

42
00:02:38,086 --> 00:02:46,257
And a reason for every invalid trip: ends before it starts, zero distance, negative total, or a pickup outside its file month.

43
00:02:47,357 --> 00:02:48,445
Four new nodes.

44
00:02:48,725 --> 00:02:51,989
The intermediate model, fed by staging and two seeds.

45
00:02:52,269 --> 00:02:56,557
Then the fact table, the zone dimension, and the daily summary that uses both.

46
00:02:57,657 --> 00:02:59,364
A word on selecting models.

47
00:02:59,644 --> 00:03:01,222
A name selects one model.

48
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:03,763
A plus before it adds everything upstream.

49
00:03:04,043 --> 00:03:06,369
A plus after it adds everything downstream.

50
00:03:06,649 --> 00:03:10,297
You can also select by folder, by source, or by test type.

51
00:03:11,397 --> 00:03:20,058
We run dbt build for everything up to the daily summary: seeds, staging, intermediate, marts, and every test, in the right order.

52
00:03:20,338 --> 00:03:24,754
Then we look at the lineage, the timings in Run History, and preview the result.

53
00:03:25,854 --> 00:03:31,230
We ask the data why trips are invalid, by reason, and how many duplicates were removed.

54
00:03:31,510 --> 00:03:36,140
Every problem we found in section one now has a rule, a name, and a count.

55
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:38,916
The fact table only contains valid trips.
